    "3:ה": "(23) If it has crevices — and for this reason Acharonim agreed that a knife whose handle is attached with small nails — hagalah does not help, for there are many crevices at the place of the nails; how much more so if the body of the handle is made from two glued parts with a slight gap between them to insert the knife blade — for usually one cannot scrape there well. Sometimes there is a flaw between the blade and handle and one cannot clean that place well unless one removes the handle from the blade [Peri Megadim]. And if the knife handle is glued to the grip with adhesive — hagalah does not help, for since the adhesive deteriorates in boiling water, one must be concerned lest he be cautious with the adhesive and not boil the water well; likewise if the handle is made of horn — hagalah is not effective, for horn deteriorates in boiling water and one must be concerned lest he be cautious with it [Acharonim].",
    "3:ו": "(24) And requires libun — meaning one places burning coals on that place; and with libun one need not remove rust, for fire burns everything.",
    "3:ז": "(h) In the place of the riots, it will be done before the rise and if it does not do so before, it will be done after the rise:",
    "3:ח": "(26) It has no remedy through hagalah — because one cannot scrape it well from inside unless one opens the stitching and scrapes well, and then even hagalah is not required. A vessel whose mouth is narrow and has rust there that cannot be removed — hagalah does not help for it [Acharonim]. They also wrote that anywhere hagalah is required, peeling with artisan tools does not help, for absorption exits from wall to wall, and artisan tools help only where peeling is required.",
    "4:א": "(27) Through fire — meaning without water, such as a spit and skewer on which one roasts chametz directly [and even though when roasting on meat they coat it with fat or grease the face with fat, this slight moisture does not prevent the fire from fully dominating it, and it is not comparable to a pan explained below in seif 11]; and since the body of the chametz touches them during roasting and there is no liquid between them to carry chametz taste into the spit and skewer except through fire heat alone — chametz taste is absorbed in them; therefore it is not expelled from them through hagalah in boiling water either, except through fire heat, for as it absorbs so it expels. But if one knows he did not roast chametz on it, only that he roasted meat with unsifted salt — hagalah suffices, even l'chatchila.",
    "4:ב": "(28) Require libun — I will precede with a brief introduction and will need it in several places. Know that the Gemara teaches that specifically prohibited matter absorbed in a vessel through fire requires libun; but permitted matter absorbed through fire, even if later forbidden — such as kodshim meat roasted on a spit over fire, even if ultimately the absorption in the spit became notar — nevertheless kashering that spit requires only hagalah; and poskim dispute regarding chametz absorbed in a vessel: some say this too is called hittira belia, for when chametz of the roast was absorbed in the spit during the year it was permitted, and therefore hagalah suffices; according to them, all vessels whose use is through fire for Pesach need only hagalah. Others say: specifically notar at the time of absorption — there was no notar on the meat at all, therefore it is called hittira belia; but chametz — at the time the spit was used with chametz, chametz was on the absorption, and now too it is called chametz, only now the prohibition was retroactively aroused on account of this chametz — this is not called hittira belia, for chametz is absorbed within and it is still chametz and falls under issura belia, and libun is required. Most poskim agreed to this, and therefore the Mechaber ruled like them; nevertheless one should not infer from this law to other places, for sometimes where there are other grounds to be lenient we combine also the view of poskim who hold chametz is called hittira belia.",
